        "problem": "$\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{\u2032},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)\\, & \u27f9\\,{u}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\, & \u27f9\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x(2\\sqrt{x})-\\int (2\\sqrt{x})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)-2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)-4\\sqrt{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.",
